data: {"id":"chatcmpl-20261007052812830628300c955d568e8LHbdw4","created":1791350893,"object":"chat.completion.chunk","model":"glm-5.3-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_69f2f55ba33441b9a522a497","index":0,"type":"function","function":{"name":"write","arguments":"{\"content\":\"# Deadlock Resolution Summary\\n\\n## Setup\\n\\n`deadlock_sim.py` runs two threads against `migration.db`, each with its own\\nSQLite connection, updating two accounts in opposite orders:\\n\\n| Thread | Update order                     |\\n|--------|----------------------------------|\\n| A      | account 1 -> sleep -> account 2  |\\n| B      | account 2 -> sleep -> account 1  |\\n\\nEach thread opens a write transaction, updates one account, then sleeps for\\n0.1s while still holding the transaction open. The sleep widens the race\\nwindow so the collision reproduces reliably.\\n\\n## Cause of the deadlock\\n\\nThe two threads form a circular wait:\\n\\n```mermaid\\ngraph LR\\n    A[Thread A] -- \\\"holds tx on\\\" --> R1[account 1]\\n    B[Thread B] -- \\\"holds tx on\\\" --> R2[account 2]\\n    A -- \\\"wants\\\" --> R2\\n    B -- \\\"wants\\\" --> R1\\n```\\n\\n1. Thread A updates account 1, Thread B updates account 2; each keeps its\\n   transaction open across the sleep.\\n2. A now needs account 2 (held by B) while B needs account 1 (held by A).\\n3. Neither can proceed until the other commits: a classic deadlock caused by\\n   **inconsistent lock ordering** (opposite update orders).\\n\\nSQLite has no row-level locks, so it cannot detect and park the deadlock\\nindefinitely. It resolves it by rejecting one of the two writers immediately\\nwith `SQLITE_BUSY`, surfaced in Python as:\\n\\n```\\nsqlite3.OperationalError: database is locked\\n```\\n\\nNote: a busy timeout longer than the held-transaction sleep hides the\\ndeadlock entirely (the loser just waits out the winner). The script uses a\\n0.05s timeout, deliberately shorter than the 0.1s sleep, so the collision\\nsurfaces as a real error.\\n\\n## Retry strategy\\n\\nThe losing thread treats the lock error as transient and retries the\\n**entire transaction**, not just the failed statement:\\n\\n1. Catch `sqlite3.OperationalError` containing \\\"database is locked\\\".\\n2. `ROLLBACK` the whole transaction so no partial writes linger.\\n3. Wait a short randomized delay: 0.2s base plus up to 0.1s jitter\\n   (jitter prevents the two threads from retrying in lockstep).\\n4. Re-run the full transaction: `BEGIN`, both updates, `COMMIT`.\\n5. Up to 3 retries (`MAX_RETRIES = 3`); non-lock errors and exhausted\\n   retries fail the thread cleanly.\\n\\nRetrying the whole transaction matters: the first attempt left partially\\napplied updates (only one account incremented), which must not be committed.\\n\\n## Result\\n\\nThe deadlock still occurs on the first pass by design, but recovery is\\ndeterministic: by the first retry the other thread has committed and released\\nits lock, so the retried transaction succeeds.\\n\\n```\\n[A] BEGIN (attempt 1); updating account 1\\n[B] BEGIN (attempt 1); updating account 2\\n[B] DEADLOCK on attempt 1: database is locked; rolling back, retrying in 0.28s\\n[A] COMMIT\\n[B] BEGIN (attempt 2) ... [B] COMMIT\\n\\nThread A: committed (attempt 1)\\nThread B: committed (attempt 2)\\nVERIFICATION: PASS - both transactions committed, no unresolved deadlock\\n```\\n\\nVerified over multiple runs: both threads always commit, exit code 0.\\n\\n## Alternative: prevent rather than retry\\n\\nRetries recover from the deadlock but still waste a transaction on every\\ncollision. The cleaner fix is **consistent lock ordering**: have both threads\\nupdate accounts in the same order (e.g. always 1 then 2). A circular wait\\nthen becomes impossible and the retries would never trigger.\\n\",\"file_path\":\"/home/agent/workspace/deadlock_resolution.md\",\"intent\":\"Save deadlock cause and retry strategy summary as deadlock_resolution.md\"}"}}]}}]}

